Hello, this is Sumanth. Today marks Day 8 of my Cloud Architect journey.
Date : 19-05-2026
Day : Tuesday

GOAL:
Deep dive into Linux user management, permissions, process control, and build a real interactive Linux administration automation script.

====================================================
LINUX USER MANAGEMENT
====================================================

1. Create New User

Command:
sudo useradd username

Example:
sudo useradd devuser

Purpose:
Create a new Linux user account.


2. Set Password

Command:
sudo passwd username

Example:
sudo passwd devuser

Output:
New password:
Retype new password:
passwd: password updated successfully

Purpose:
Assign password to a Linux user.


3. Delete User

Command:
sudo userdel username

Example:
sudo userdel devuser

Purpose:
Delete an existing user.


4. Delete User with Home Directory

Command:
sudo userdel -r username

Example:
sudo userdel -r devuser

Purpose:
Delete user along with home directory and files.


5. Add User to sudo Group

Command:
sudo usermod -aG sudo username

Example:
sudo usermod -aG sudo devuser

Check:
groups devuser

Output:
devuser : devuser sudo

Purpose:
Grant administrator privileges.


6. Switch User

Commands:
su username
sudo su
sudo -i

Examples:
su devuser
sudo su

Purpose:
Switch between users or become root user.


====================================================
LINUX PERMISSIONS DEEP DIVE
====================================================

7. File Ownership

Commands:
ls -l
chown

Examples:
ls -l
sudo chown sumanth:sumanth file.txt

Purpose:
Manage file ownership.


8. Symbolic chmod

Examples:
chmod u+x script.sh
chmod g+w file.txt
chmod o-r secret.txt

Meaning:
u = user
g = group
o = others
r = read
w = write
x = execute

Purpose:
Change permissions symbolically.


9. Numeric chmod

Examples:
chmod 755 deploy.sh
chmod 644 config.txt
chmod 700 private.sh

Meaning:
7 = rwx
6 = rw-
5 = r-x
4 = r--
0 = ---

Purpose:
Change permissions numerically.


10. Special Permissions

setuid

Command:
chmod u+s filename

Example:
chmod u+s myscript.sh

Purpose:
Run file with owner's privileges.


setgid

Command:
chmod g+s directory

Example:
chmod g+s shared/

Purpose:
Files inherit group ownership.


sticky bit

Command:
chmod +t directory

Example:
chmod +t /tmp

Purpose:
Only file owner can delete files in shared directories.


====================================================
PROCESS MANAGEMENT
====================================================

11. View Running Processes

Commands:
ps
ps aux
top
htop

Examples:
ps aux
top

Purpose:
Monitor active system processes.


12. Find Specific Process

Command:
pgrep process_name

Example:
pgrep nginx

Purpose:
Find process ID quickly.


13. Kill Process by PID

Commands:
kill PID
kill -9 PID

Examples:
kill 2345
kill -9 2345

Purpose:
Stop running processes.


14. Kill Process by Name

Command:
pkill process_name

Example:
pkill nginx

Purpose:
Kill process using process name.


15. Process Priority

Commands:
nice
renice

Examples:
nice -n 10 sleep 100
renice 5 2345

Purpose:
Manage CPU scheduling priority.


====================================================
BACKGROUND JOB CONTROL
====================================================

16. Run in Background

Command:
command &

Example:
sleep 300 &

Purpose:
Run process in background.


17. Keep Running After Logout

Command:
nohup command &

Example:
nohup python app.py &

Purpose:
Keep process running after terminal closes.


18. Detach Process

Command:
disown

Purpose:
Detach process from shell session.


19. View Jobs

Command:
jobs

Purpose:
See background jobs.


20. Foreground Job

Command:
fg

Purpose:
Bring background job to foreground.


21. Resume Background Job

Command:
bg

Purpose:
Resume stopped process in background.


====================================================
PORT & PROCESS TROUBLESHOOTING
====================================================

22. Check Port Usage

Command:
ss -tulnp | grep :80

Examples:
ss -tulnp | grep :22
ss -tulnp | grep :443
ss -tulnp | grep :3306

Purpose:
Check which process is using a specific port.


====================================================
BUILD PROJECT
====================================================

23. Interactive Linux Administration Script

Create:
user-management.sh

Code:

#!/bin/bash

while true
do
 echo "===== USER MANAGEMENT MENU ====="
 echo "1. Create User"
 echo "2. Add User to sudo Group"
 echo "3. Set Password"
 echo "4. List Users"
 echo "5. Delete User"
 echo "6. Exit"

 read -p "Choose option: " choice

 case $choice in
   1)
     read -p "Enter username: " username
     sudo useradd "$username"
     echo "User created successfully"
     ;;

   2)
     read -p "Enter username: " username
     sudo usermod -aG sudo "$username"
     echo "User added to sudo group"
     ;;

   3)
     read -p "Enter username: " username
     sudo passwd "$username"
     ;;

   4)
     cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd
     ;;

   5)
     read -p "Enter username: " username
     sudo userdel -r "$username"
     echo "User deleted successfully"
     ;;

   6)
     echo "Exiting..."
     exit
     ;;

   *)
     echo "Invalid option"
     ;;
 esac

done


====================================================
RUN SCRIPT
====================================================

chmod +x user-management.sh
./user-management.sh


====================================================
GIT COMMANDS
====================================================

git add .
git commit -m "Day 8 Linux deep dive users permissions processes"
git push


====================================================
KEY TAKEAWAYS
====================================================

Linux user management is critical for cloud administration.
Permissions directly impact security.
Process control is essential for troubleshooting.
Background job handling is useful for automation.
Port troubleshooting helps identify service conflicts.
Interactive bash scripting improves real-world Linux administration skills.

Day 8 focused on practical Linux administration fundamentals required for Cloud & DevOps engineering.